If there are multiple bricks that the ball collides with, it will call `resolveBrickCollision` for each.
If it hits two bricks, it might flip the velocity twice, effectively canceling the bounce.

This is the "neighbours" issue mentioned in `ISSU_md`: "the bounce must apply exactly once (`vy = -vy` applied twice cancels out and the ball goes through)".

To fix this, I should only resolve the collision if the ball is actually moving *towards* the brick's face.

Let's refine `resolveBrickCollision` to check the velocity.

```javascript
function resolveBrickCollision(ball, brick) {
  const dx1 = ball.x + ball.r - brick.x;
  const dx2 = brick.x + brick.w - (ball.x - ball.r);
  const dy1 = ball.y + ball.r - brick.y;
  const dy2 = brick.y + brick.h - (ball.y - ball.r);

  const penX = Math.min(dx1, dx2);
  const penY = Math.min(dy1, dy2);

  if (penX < penY) {
    if (dx1 < dx2) { // Hit left face
      if (ball.vx > 0) {
        ball.vx = -Math.abs(ball.vx);
        ball.x = brick.x - ball.r;
      }
    } else { // Hit right face
      if (ball.vx < 0) {
        ball.vx = Math.abs(ball.vx);
        ball.x = brick.x + brick.w + ball.r;
      }
    }
  } else if (penX > penY) {
    if (dy1 < dy2) { // Hit top face
      if (ball.vy > 0) {
        ball.vy = -Math.abs(ball.vy);
        ball.y = brick.y - ball.r;
      }
    } else { // Hit bottom face
      if (ball.vy < 0) {
        ball.vy = Math.abs(ball.vy);
        ball.y = brick.y + brick.h + ball.r;
      }
    }
  } else {
    // Corner hit: flip both only if moving towards it
    // This is trickier, but typically we flip both if it's moving into the corner
    ball.vx = -ball.vx;
    ball.vy = -ball.vy;
    if (dx1 < dx2) ball.x = brick.x - ball.r; else ball.x = brick.x + brick.w + ball.r;
    if (dy1 < dy2) ball.y = brick.y - ball.r; else ball.y = brick.y + brick.h + ball.r;
  }
